  "nameStory": "",
  "description": "Hairy Long Stem Marasmius (Rhizomarasmius pyrrhocephalus) is a small, delicate mushroom characterized by its distinctive reddish-brown to burgundy cap and exceptionally long, thin, hair-like stem. This species belongs to the Physalacriaceae family and is known for its remarkable ability to revive after drying out.",
  "idTips": [
    {
      "label": "Reddish-Brown Cap",
      "description": "The cap is small (typically 0.5-1.5 cm) with a distinctive reddish-brown or burgundy coloration that fades slightly with age",
      "isPrimary": true
    },
    {
      "label": "Extremely Long, Thin Stem",
      "description": "The stem is disproportionately long relative to the cap size, appearing hair-like and wiry, often 5-10 cm tall",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Sparse, Distant Gills",
      "description": "Gills are well-spaced and whitish to pale, attached or slightly free from the stem",
      "isPrimary": false
    }
  ],
  "habitat": "This fungus is found in moist woodlands and forests throughout New England, typically growing on decaying hardwood leaves, moss, and plant debris on the forest floor.",
  "seasonality": {
    "spring": "",
    "summer": "",
    "fall": "",
    "winter": ""
  },
  "funFact": "Like other marasmius species, Rhizomarasmius pyrrhocephalus is remarkable for its ability to undergo reversible desiccation—it can dry out completely and revive when moisture returns, making it a hardy survivor in variable forest conditions.",
